---
import Solution from '../../layouts/Solution.astro';
import SolutionExample from '../../components/SolutionExample.astro';
import SolutionStarters from '../../components/SolutionStarters.astro';
import SolutionFit from '../../components/SolutionFit.astro';
import SolutionFaq from '../../components/SolutionFaq.astro';
import DocsHandoff from '../../components/DocsHandoff.astro';
import { solutionAreas, sharedFamilyFaq } from '../../data/solutions';
import { askQuestionHref, contactEmail } from '../../data/contact';
const area = solutionAreas.find((a) => a.key === 'business-and-tax')!;
---
<Solution
  area={area}
  heading="Keep the register of businesses that other services check."
  description="Annual filings arrive faster than they are entered, a company with three registrations has three files, and the procurement teams and banks that need to know whether a business is active ask your staff instead of your register. Registry Stack gives your office one register of the businesses and taxpayers it has registered, and lets other services check a registration without seeing the file."
>
  <SolutionExample
    eyebrow="One example: a supplier registration check"
    heading="Tell a procurement team whether a supplier is active."
    intro="A government procurement team needs to know whether a supplier is actively registered before it processes the supplier’s tender. Your register can answer that question without sending the supplier’s file."
    items={[
      { term: 'Who asks', body: 'The procurement team, for the supplier named on the tender.' },
      { term: 'What the office wants', body: 'The procurement team learns whether the supplier is active or inactive, in an answer it can check came from your register, and learns nothing else.' },
      { term: 'What happens', body: 'The procurement team asks Evidence Gateway. The gateway confirms your office has agreed this team may ask, reads the status from your register, and returns the answer. The procurement team decides what it means for the tender.' },
      { term: 'What stays with your office', body: 'Filing history, addresses, and ownership details stay in your register.' },
    ]}
    link={{ href: '/products/evidence-gateway/#product-example', label: 'See the supplier check step by step' }}
  />
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="business-change-title">
    <div class="section-inner">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">What changes for your office</p>
        <h2 id="business-change-title">One entry per business, and extracts your staff stop preparing by hand.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="product-facts">
        <article><h3>A business is entered once, however many registrations it holds</h3><p>The business is one entry. Its company registration, its tax registration, and each authorization it holds are linked to it, so your staff look up one business and see everything registered against it.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Owners are looked up only by those you name</h3><p>Who owns a business is personal information. Your office names who may look owners up and under which legal duty, and they look up a business they already know; nobody pages through the list.</p></article>
        <article><h3>A filing keeps its history from receipt to publication</h3><p>Your team records a filing on the day it arrives, then records its review and its publication as each is decided. The earlier status stays visible, so the register shows when a filing was received and when it was published.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <SolutionStarters
    area={area}
    heading="Start from a business or tax register like yours."
    intro="A starter sets out what a register of this kind holds and who may change it, for one common register of businesses, taxpayers, filings, or financial rights. Your team takes the closest one and adapts it to its own law."
  />
  <SolutionFit
    area={area}
    heading="Keep the register where it is, or build it new."
    jobs={{
      'base-registry': 'Holds the register of businesses, taxpayers, or filings, under your rules for who may record and look up each one.',
      'evidence-gateway': 'Answers a procurement team’s or bank’s question about a business, such as whether it is actively registered, and nothing more.',
    }}
    extra={[
      { term: 'Your registration process keeps deciding', body: 'Applications, fees, and the decision to register a business or a taxpayer stay in the systems that handle them today. The register records a registration once your office has decided it.' },
      { term: 'If your office is a central bank or financial supervisor', body: 'The bank account, credit and collateral starters follow the same rules, with lookups limited to named roles and no balances or repayments recorded.' },
    ]}
    muted
  >
    <p>Each product runs on its own. A tax administration with a working register often starts by letting services check a status from it; a new register of beneficial owners often starts with the register itself.</p>
  </SolutionFit>
  <SolutionFaq
    items={[
      {
        q: 'Does this replace our business registration system?',
        a: 'It depends on what holds your register today. If a registration system takes applications, collects fees and issues the registration, that system stays and records the result in the register. If your register is a spreadsheet, or lives inside an application you are retiring, Base Registry Engine can be the register, with your team planning the migration of the existing entries.',
        link: { href: 'https://docs.registrystack.org/explanation/integration-patterns/', label: 'How it sits beside existing systems' },
      },
      {
        q: 'Can a bank check a business is registered without an account on our system?',
        a: 'Two cases. A member of the public or a bank looking up one business uses the public register your application publishes under your law. A service that needs to check businesses as part of its own process asks your register a question you have agreed to answer, such as whether a business is actively registered, and gets that answer and nothing else. Your office decides which services may ask, and which questions.',
        link: { href: '/solutions/check-a-fact/', label: 'How a service checks a fact' },
      },
      {
        q: 'Who can see who owns a business?',
        a: 'Your office decides, role by role: who may record an owner, who may look one up, and under which legal duty. Every look-up and every change is kept with who did it and when.',
        link: { href: '/security/', label: 'Who is responsible for what' },
      },
      {
        q: 'What is a starter, exactly?',
        a: 'A free package requested from the starter’s page and opened to see how a register of this kind is set out: the records it keeps, the changes staff can make, and who may make them. It is a starting point to adapt, not a running system: your team adds what your register needs, and using one does not mean the register is in use anywhere. Anyone from the office can request it; your technical team is who opens it.',
        link: { href: '/starters/#business-and-tax', label: 'Browse the business and tax starters' },
      },
      {
        q: 'Can we still sell certificates and extracts?',
        a: 'Yes. The register holds the entries; what your office charges for, and how it issues a certificate, stays with your office and its application.',
      },
      {
        q: 'Where does our technical team start?',
        a: 'If you keep your register where it is: the Evidence Gateway tutorial, which answers a question from sample data on a laptop. If you are building or moving a register: the Base Registry Engine tutorial, then the starter closest to your register.',
        link: { href: 'https://docs.registrystack.org/tutorials/first-breg/', label: 'Run the registry tutorial' },
      },
      ...sharedFamilyFaq,
    ]}
  />
  <DocsHandoff
    eyebrow="Work with us"
    heading="Talk it through with the people who build it."
    body="Tell us which register you keep and what other services ask of it. We reply by email, and a short call is the usual next step. Paid pilots and support are there if you want them."
    primaryLabel="Ask a question"
    primaryHref={askQuestionHref(area.name)}
    emphasis="docs"
    note={`Or write to ${contactEmail}.`}
  />
</Solution>
